title:: BlipExtensions
summary:: Finite-harmonic Blip transformations and pulse-shape oscillators
categories:: Guides>BiroSCa
related:: Classes/Blip, Classes/Pulse, Classes/SinOscFB

description::

BlipExtensions is an experimental BiroSCa family derived from the finite
harmonic structure of SuperCollider's link::Classes/Blip::. It does not
replace the core oscillator. The native implementations use double-precision
phase, a small Nyquist margin, analytic singular limits, deterministic invalid
input handling, and smooth topology changes.

The source architecture was studied in SuperCollider's
code::server/plugins/OscUGens.cpp::, including the historical Blip, Pulse, and
SinOscFB implementations. The new code retains the useful finite-kernel and
one-sample-state ideas while replacing the legacy fixed-point
sine/cosecant-table evaluator.

subsection:: Spectral structures

link::Classes/BlipSeries:: places partials on an arithmetic frequency series.
link::Classes/BlipKernel:: applies rectangular, triangular, or efficient
cosine-power weights. link::Classes/BlipPeak:: is a nonnegative Fejér pulse.
link::Classes/BlipDecay:: applies exponential decay, and
link::Classes/BlipFrac:: fades the final partial continuously.

subsection:: Pulse and phase structures

link::Classes/BlipWidth:: separates width from harmonic count using a
re-anchored stateful reconstruction. link::Classes/BlipAD:: filters a finite
impulse train with two one-pole responses. link::Classes/BlipDisp:: applies
fixed quadratic phase, while link::Classes/BlipPM:: applies a shared
sample-accurate phase displacement.

subsection:: Calculus, waveshaping, and feedback

link::Classes/BlipDiff:: is a normalized phase derivative and
link::Classes/BlipInt:: uses inverse-harmonic integration weights.
link::Classes/BlipPower:: is a deliberately simple language-side nonlinear
composition. link::Classes/BlipFB:: provides true one-sample normalized phase
feedback.

subsection:: Complexity and aliasing

All native classes except BlipDisp and BlipInt have constant per-sample cost
with respect to harmonic count. BlipDisp and BlipInt are intentionally O(N)
and stop at 2048 requested harmonics. Carrier limiting cannot suppress
sidebands created by BlipPM, nonlinear content created by BlipPower, or
feedback products created by BlipFB.

